✅ Combined: Pan + Dynamic Zoom (Zoom IN while Panning)

This is the most cinematic version — it pans right while simultaneously zooming in from 1.2x to 1.5x, like a classic Ken Burns effect.

import subprocess

def pan_right_with_zoom_in(
    input_image: str,
    output_video: str,
    start_zoom: float = 1.2,
    end_zoom: float = 1.5,
    duration_sec: int = 4,
    fps: int = 25,
    width: int = 1920,
    height: int = 1080,
):
    total_frames = duration_sec * fps
    zoom_delta = end_zoom - start_zoom  # positive = zoom in

Zoom increases linearly from start_zoom to end_zoom
    zoom_expr = f"{start_zoom}+({zoom_delta}*(on/{total_frames}))"

x pans right — uses current zoom value for safe bounding
Since zoom changes per frame, we use the same dynamic expression
    x_expr = f"(iw-(iw/({zoom_expr})))*(on/{total_frames})"

y stays vertically centered
    y_expr = f"ih/2-(ih/({zoom_expr})/2)"

    zoompan_filter = (
        f"zoompan="
        f"z='{zoom_expr}':"
        f"x='{x_expr}':"
        f"y='{y_expr}':"
        f"d={total_frames}:"
        f"s={width}x{height}:"
        f"fps={fps}"
    )

    command = [
        "ffmpeg",
        "-loop", "1",
        "-i", input_image,
        "-vf", zoompan_filter,
        "-c:v", "libx264",
        "-crf", "18",
        "-preset", "slow",
        "-pix_fmt", "yuv420p",
        "-t", str(duration_sec),
        "-y",
        output_video,
    ]

    print("Running FFmpeg Pan-Right + Zoom-In command:")
    print(" ".join(command))

    result = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True)

    if result.returncode != 0:
        print("FFmpeg Error:\n", result.stderr)
        raise RuntimeError("FFmpeg failed.")
    else:
        print(f"✅ Pan Right + Zoom In saved to: {output_video}")

--- Usage ---
pan_right_with_zoom_in(
    input_image="input.jpg",
    output_video="pan_right_zoom_in.mp4",
    start_zoom=1.2,
    end_zoom=1.5,
    duration_sec=4,
    fps=25,
    width=1920,
    height=1080,
)

📊 Effect Comparison Summary

| Effect | Zoom | x Direction | y |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pan Right | Fixed (e.g. 1.3) | 0 → max_x | Centered |
| Pan Left | Fixed (e.g. 1.3) | max_x → 0 | Centered |
| Pan Right + Zoom In | 1.2 → 1.5 (dynamic) | 0 → max_x (dynamic) | Centered |
| Pan Left + Zoom Out | 1.5 → 1.2 (dynamic) | max_x → 0 (dynamic) | Centered |

📌 Critical Reminders

Zoom must always be > 1.0** when panning — at zoom 1.0, max_x = 0 and there is literally no pixel space to move across.
Input image resolution* must be large enough — at zoom 1.3 with 1920×1080 output, your image should be at least *~2496×1404px** to avoid any upscaling artifacts.
To make Pan Left + Zoom Out, simply flip start_zoom/end_zoom in the combined script and reverse the x_expr direction using (1-(on/{total_frames})).